.\" tesseract-crypt(1) — man page for the tesseract-crypt command line tool
.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.TH TESSERACT-CRYPT 1 "October 2026" "tesseract-crypt 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tesseract-crypt \- encrypt and decrypt strings and files with public/private keys
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tesseract-crypt
\fIcommand\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B tscrypt
\fIcommand\fR [\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tesseract-crypt
is the command-line interface for
.BR libtesseract_crypt ,
a C11 library for hybrid public-key encryption with forward secrecy,
Ed25519 signatures (sign-then-encrypt), per-chunk XChaCha20-Poly1305,
and an Argon2id passphrase fallback mode.
.PP
The binary is installed under two names: the canonical name
.B tesseract-crypt
and the short alias
.BR tscrypt .
Both names are identical and available on every platform
(Linux, Windows, macOS, BSD). On Windows the
.B .exe
suffix is added automatically.
.PP
Default input/output is stdin/stdout. Output to a terminal (TTY) is
automatically wrapped in the text armor
.B "-----BEGIN TESSERACT MESSAGE-----"
so that it can be copied and pasted safely. Armored input is auto-detected
during decryption and inspection.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B keygen
\fR\-o\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB--passphrase\fR] [\fB--kdf-ops\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB--kdf-mem\fR \fIMB\fR]
Create the key pair \fINAME.key\fR (private) and \fINAME.pub\fR (public).
With \fB--passphrase\fR the private key is stored locked
(XChaCha20-Poly1305 with an Argon2id-derived key).
.TP
.B pubkey
\fB\-k\fR \fIKEYFILE\fR [\fB\-o\fR \fIOUT\fR]
Export the public key from a private key file.
.TP
.B encrypt
\fB\-r\fR \fIRECIPIENT.pub\fR ...
Encrypt a file for a recipient. Repeat
\fB\-r\fR to send to several recipients at once (format v2).
Without \fB\-k\fR the message is anonymous;
with \fB\-k\fR the message is signed (Ed25519).
.TP
.B encrypt
\fB--passphrase\fR [\fB\-i\fR \fIIN\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIOUT\fR] [\fB--armor\fR]
Symmetric mode: the key is derived from a passphrase with Argon2id.
.TP
.B encrypt
\fB--text\fR \(dqsecret\(dq \fB\-r\fR \fIRECIPIENT.pub\fR
Encrypt a string directly; the output is armored to stdout.
.TP
.B decrypt
\fB\-k\fR \fIRECIPIENT.key\fR [\fB\-i\fR \fIIN\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIOUT\fR] [\fB--require-signer\fR \fISIGNER.pub\fR]
Decrypt a message. With \fB--require-signer\fR the signature is verified
against that public key before any plaintext is written (identity pinning).
.TP
.B decrypt
\fB--passphrase\fR [\fB\-i\fR \fIIN\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIOUT\fR]
Symmetric mode: enter the same passphrase that was used for encryption.
.TP
.B sign
\fB\-k\fR \fIKEY\fR [\fB\-i\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIFILE.sig\fR]
Sign a file (detached Ed25519ph signature).
.TP
.B verify
\fB\-k\fR \fIPUBKEY\fR \fB\-i\fR \fIFILE\fR \fB\-s\fR \fIFILE.sig\fR
Verify a detached signature.
.TP
.B rekey
\fB\-k\fR \fIOLD_KEY\fR \fR|\fR \fB\-\-old-passphrase\fR \fIPASS\fR [\fB\-i\fR \fIIN\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIOUT\fR] \fB\-r\fR \fIRECIPIENT.pub\fR [\fB\-r\fR \fIMORE.pub\fR ...] [\fB\-s\fR \fISENDER.key\fR] [\fB\-\-require-signer\fR \fISIGNER.pub\fR] [\fB\-\-chunk-size\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-no-sign\fR]
Re-encrypt a message for new recipients (key rotation); may change the sender and verify the previous signer.
.TP
.B inspect
[\fB\-i\fR \fIFILE\fR]
Read the message header (version, mode, chunk size, plaintext length,
recipient count, and signature status) without decrypting.
.TP
.B info
\fB\-k\fR \fIKEYFILE\fR
Show the key file type and its fingerprint.
.TP
.B version
Print the version. Equivalent to \fB--version\fR / \fB\-V\fR.
.TP
.B help
Print help. Equivalent to \fB--help\fR / \fB\-h\fR.
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-i \fIFILE\fR
Input file; \fI\-\fR or omitted means stdin.
.TP
.B \-o \fIFILE\fR
Output file; \fI\-\fR or omitted means stdout.
.TP
.B \-\-armor
Force armored text output (default when writing to a TTY).
.TP
.B \-\-chunk-size \fIN\fR
Plaintext chunk size per block; range 4096\ ...\ 16777216 bytes
(default 65536).
.TP
.B \-\-kdf-ops \fIN\fR
and
.B \-\-kdf-mem \fIMB\fR
Adjust the Argon2id cost for key files (keygen) or passphrase
mode (encrypt/decrypt).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
General error (I/O, file format, library error code).
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: unknown or incomplete arguments (some argument validation
errors also map here).
.TP
.B 3
Authentication failure: AEAD, signature, required signer, recipient mismatch, or wrong passphrase.
No output file was written.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tesseract-crypt keygen -o alice --passphrase
tesseract-crypt keygen -o bob
tesseract-crypt encrypt -r bob.pub -k alice.key -i secret.txt -o secret.enc
tesseract-crypt decrypt -k bob.key -i secret.enc -o secret.out --require-signer alice.pub
tesseract-crypt encrypt --text "Hello world" -r bob.pub
tesseract-crypt rekey -k bob.key -i secret.enc -o secret.rekey -r charlie.pub
.f
.PP
In every example above, the
.B tesseract-crypt
prefix can be replaced with
.BR tscrypt .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B NAME.key
Private key. TSK1 (164 bytes, raw) or TSK2 (232 bytes, Argon2id-locked).
May be armored with the label
.B "TESSERACT PRIVATE KEY"
or
.BR "TESSERACT PRIVATE KEY (LOCKED)" .
.TP
.B NAME.pub
Public key TSKp (68 bytes); armor label
.BR "TESSERACT PUBLIC KEY" .
.SH AVAILABILITY
Built and tested cross-platform: Windows, Linux, macOS, BSD/Unix-like.
The \fBtscrypt\fR alias is available on every platform (with the automatic
\fB.exe\fR suffix on Windows).
.SH SECURITY
Private keys are \fBsodium_mlock\fR-ed when loaded and \fBsodium_memzero\fR-ed
after use. The 264-byte header is the AAD of every chunk, so truncation,
reordering, and splicing are detected cryptographically. Argon2id parameters
from untrusted headers are capped (maximum 16 ops and 4 GiB)
to prevent memory abuse. The built-in libsodium RNG
(\fBrandombytes_buf\fR) is always used \-\- this code never rolls its own
RNG. Atomic file replacement uses rename; on Windows it uses
\fBMoveFileExA\fR to overwrite the destination.
.sp
See DEVELOPMENT.md and docs/format.html for the full specification,
and SECURITY.md for how to report vulnerabilities.
